# LIVE REGRESSION FIXTURE (graduated 2026-08-29) — a user method's NAME used to
# decide memory safety.
#
# WHAT IT WAS. Two byte-identical programs, differing only in what the mutator
# is called:
#     void grow(&self):   ...  ->  SIGSEGV, rc 139, on C AND LLVM
#     void resize(&self): ...  ->  rc 0, prints "helloworld", correct
#
# `resize` was on a hardcoded 25-entry `MUTATING_METHODS` list in
# `src/ir/lowering/functions.rs`; `grow` was not. That list fed
# `is_mutating_method_name` as the fallback beside the typed builtin predicate,
# and the CoW prescan used it to decide whether a receiver is mutated. A user
# `&self` method whose NAME was absent was INVISIBLE to the prescan, so the
# bind was never materialized and the later realloc freed the buffer the bind
# still pointed at. CLAUDE.md's "No name matching" rule violated with a
# memory-safety consequence -- not a style nit; the const's own doc-comment
# already conceded it ("this list survives only as the escape hatch").
#
# WHAT IT IS NOW. The prescan reads the semantic pass's typed per-receiver
# classification (`semantic::safety::ReceiverMutations`, resolved from the
# receiver's type and the method's declared `&self`), and both the list and its
# self-host mirror are deleted. This file asserts `helloworld` and is wired
# LIVE; the corpus-scanned sibling that carries BOTH names in one program is
# `tests/fixtures/cow_user_mutator_rename_invariance.gg`, and the ASan cell is
# `tests/fixtures/security/sound_user_mutator_name_invariant_uaf.gg`.
#
# It stays in `known_gaps/` (out of `runtime_parity_corpus`) for placement
# reasons only -- moving it would register a corpus row for a shape the corpus
# already covers.
#
# ⚠ THE REALLOC IS LOAD-BEARING IN THIS REPRO. `grow` must push enough to
# force the backing store to reallocate (64x here). A version that pushes once
# is rc 0 on BOTH names and proves nothing -- the orchestrator's first probe
# made exactly that mistake. Keep the loop.
#
# ⊕ CORRECTED 2026-08-29: THE HEAP-FORCED PAYLOAD IS *NOT* LOAD-BEARING. This
# header used to say "keep the payload heap-FORCED (built via `mk`, never a
# literal)" beside the realloc requirement, as though both were necessary.
# Measured on the pre-fix compiler, on this exact shape:
#     literal payload + the 64-push loop  -> rc 139  (still reproduces)
#     `mk` payload    + a single push     -> rc 0    (does not reproduce)
# So the realloc is the load-bearing half and the heap payload is not. `mk` is
# kept anyway because it makes the freed element a heap String, which is what
# turns a bare SIGSEGV into a legible ASan `heap-use-after-free` in
# `gorget_string_clone_to_owned` -- useful, not required.
# ⚠ MEASURED ON THIS SHAPE ONLY. The same warning appears verbatim on
# `todo/t0703`/`t0704` and their fixtures, which are DIFFERENT defects (an
# alias-spelled view; a closure capture); nobody has run the literal-payload
# variant there, so do not propagate this correction to them unmeasured.
#
# DISTINCT FROM `t0125`, same root gap, different severity and shape -- state
# the discriminator before merging them:
#   * `t0125` is MED, LOOP-CARRIED, `Vector[int]` (NO heap payload), and
#     asserts WRONG VALUES (3,3,4,4 vs 3,2,2,4). Framed as
#     "under-materialize / per-iteration throwaway / clone-balloon risk".
#   * THIS is STRAIGHT-LINE, a HEAP element, and a hard SIGSEGV /
#     ASan heap-use-after-free on BOTH backends. Not a wrong value: a crash.
#
# ASan (C lane): `heap-use-after-free` in `gorget_string_clone_to_owned`
# called from `Holder__probe`.
#
# INTENDED (asserted below): prints `helloworld` regardless of the method's
# name, on BOTH backends.
# Measured 2026-08-27 by the R45 DEEP-1 scout; orchestrator-verified with the
# renamed control run alongside.

String mk(String a, String b):
    return a + b

struct Holder:
    Vector[String] items

equip Holder:
    void grow(&self):
        int i = 0
        while i < 64:
            self.items.push(mk("xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx", "yyyyyyyyyyyyyyyy"))
            i = i + 1

    String probe(&self):
        String s = self.items.get(0).unwrap()
        self.grow()
        return s

void main():
    Holder h = Holder([mk("hello", "world")])
    print(h.probe())
